#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515624} :activity/id #uuid "14db1be8-51e1-5036-b934-216c3bf6e275" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33978283-6287-570d-93ab-bb7631c431be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514988} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5413a60-3a3a-5440-92ee-5f361e71fff8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca58d00f-a386-5b1e-a85b-e37b24ea3447" :question/math-academy-id "q-337284" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3(t-1)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{3t}-\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ 3(t-1)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0418a620-c5b3-5d53-82c0-63ca1c0bfbbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03391546-51e2-592b-b33e-96a0e7dadad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308770} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4cc6a92-1dbb-533a-bde1-5f5375f2fb88" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c40bb4a2-ee82-5c62-8098-95674097c15b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310321} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3b02da-876b-5e0a-8cf7-c53cb24cd812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea97844-39dd-5c98-b9ff-e503c4be0f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-336699" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "082a26b1-f3c0-5b74-a962-43a65356bce3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e08da9-67bd-5515-a054-84a8357eeeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309281} {:answer/id #uuid "534c0f49-500e-5197-9bbd-6d09cb24788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309380} {:answer/id #uuid "597a5fcb-acf1-507d-a353-70f05f27cf4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309421} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9f5e84-6187-51e3-b1ba-d485dbd83ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f089efb-a288-5a25-99bb-2f2c35b14bdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9413d3-2dd0-5b9c-af86-84118da8b49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308828} {:answer/id #uuid "66dea2be-fb2a-5e49-8f24-f5bf098b70b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} {:answer/id #uuid "85bc0b4c-26c1-564c-b0cc-0d3faa27a1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309789} {:answer/id #uuid "96ddbfc5-4828-537e-9f4f-20b74f781e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} :answer-field/id #uuid "89eb1346-c61c-5d99-a38f-a8915bf8aeb6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148cbe9c-5e50-5894-8133-94a7e83eaffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} {:answer/id #uuid "aad5e34e-5cb9-538c-896a-149719edd725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310128} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4009cc-2f28-553e-b2cc-74e5a8c34f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310265} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a20eb3-b4e9-5340-8083-468f920be621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310128} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7d6706-c034-5ad7-b9c2-d78eb5a0f383" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311198} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d49db2-b24b-5622-ad80-9b08aa150e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308947} {:answer/id #uuid "45498a31-a0e4-5a19-bbd1-13164db595c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309276} {:answer/id #uuid "633a649e-40b2-5363-8abc-94d62e0cac29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309509} {:answer/id #uuid "674a326d-23d4-5dbf-818a-b08a2b7071fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b44571e6-f5cf-5a50-a359-5da2ad7dfbff" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cf3081d-968b-5f36-a1e5-bb052c96fbcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336713" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-4}} into $\\text{L1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "1464fa05-0d62-5c01-b491-e56ce5c8e2e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5b84d3b-c2a0-5c17-a951-7b667223d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310336} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75ca1ce-9660-5f2b-8e80-e3b967d7673c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a119389-1e86-5899-9ba6-43b2c31d6800" :question/math-academy-id "q-337288" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f772438-7511-5d11-8498-5f7b2fa9adee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e50ee6-2518-5d34-a3e2-4799e3d17703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309180} :answer-field/id #uuid "573e1e64-9846-5e0e-bb82-38a0518ddaaf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7781a288-b83b-5516-8d03-ce94842577fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309684} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a42318d-7765-5b03-b495-8b35312f578c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ac8d1ba-163b-54eb-addd-dba5b1956964" :question/math-academy-id "q-337280" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1f84e26-b520-572d-b1af-861828868756" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515622}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514852}]}
